\subsection{Test matrix}

Each cylinder was towed at ten speeds, $U = 0.2$--1.1~m/s in steps of
0.1~m/s, giving \NRuns{} runs in total (table~\ref{tab:summary}). The
25.4 and 50.8~mm cylinders were tested in October and November~2025, and
the 38.1~mm cylinder in March~2026. Water temperature was recorded for each
run and ranged from \TMin{} to \TMax{}~$^\circ$C.

\section{Data processing}
\label{sec:processing}

A representative force record is shown in figure~\ref{fig:timeseries}.
For each run the load-cell zero (tare) was taken as the mean voltage over
the first second, with the carriage at rest. Force was obtained as
$F(t) = [V(t) - V_0]/s$, where $s$ is the calibration sensitivity in effect
on the test date. The mean drag $\overline{F}$ was averaged over
$3 < t < 8$~s, which lies within the constant-speed portion of every tow.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.65\textwidth]{timeseries_run005.pdf}
  \caption{Drag force for the 25.4~mm cylinder at $U = 0.6$~m/s.
    Shaded regions mark the tare interval ($0 < t < 1$~s) and the averaging
    window ($3 < t < 8$~s); the dashed line is the window mean. The periodic
    fluctuation is at twice the vortex-shedding frequency.}
  \label{fig:timeseries}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Fluid properties.}
Density and kinematic viscosity were evaluated at the measured temperature
of each run, using the formulation of \citet{Tanaka2001} for density and a
Vogel-type correlation for dynamic viscosity,
$\mu = 2.414\times10^{-5}\,10^{247.8/(T-140)}$~Pa\,s with $T$ in kelvin.
This agrees with the IAPWS formulation \citep{Huber2009} to within
0.5\% over 10--20~$^\circ$C. Earlier versions of this analysis
assumed 20~$^\circ$C water, which overestimated Re by \ReShiftMin--\ReShiftMax\%.

\paragraph{Non-dimensional quantities.}
The drag coefficient is based on the submerged frontal area,
\begin{equation}
  C_D = \frac{\overline{F}}{\tfrac12 \rho U^2 D L},
\end{equation}
with $\mathrm{Re} = UD/\nu$ and the diameter Froude number
$\mathrm{Fr} = U/\sqrt{gD}$, which ranges from \FrMin{} to \FrMax.
\todo{Confirm that the load cell sees no force from the dry part of the
cylinder or the mounting (e.g., air drag, spray), or estimate it.}

\paragraph{Shedding frequency.}
The dominant frequency $f_F$ of the force fluctuation in the averaging window
was located from a Hann-windowed, zero-padded periodogram. Because the
in-line force fluctuates at twice the shedding frequency
\citep{Williamson1996}, the Strouhal number is
$\mathrm{St} = f_F D / (2U)$.

\paragraph{Quality control.}
The mean load-cell output over the last second of each record, after the
carriage has stopped, was compared with the pre-run tare. For \NGood{} of
the \NRuns{} runs the two agree to within \GoodDriftMax~mN. Run~\ExclRun{}
(50.8~mm, $U = 0.8$~m/s) ended with a zero offset of \ExclDrift~N, and its
signal rises steadily through the averaging window instead of reaching a plateau.
This is consistent with a drift of the load cell or its mounting during the
tow. The run gives $C_D = \ExclCd$, about 18\% above its neighbours, and
was excluded using the criterion $|\Delta F_0| > 0.05$~N. The run log notes
a suspected zero problem for this run. \todo{Repeat this run if possible.}

\paragraph{Uncertainty.}
The force fluctuation is dominated by the periodic shedding component.
The random uncertainty of each $\overline{F}$ was therefore estimated as
$\sigma_F/\sqrt{N_\mathrm{eff}}$, where $\sigma_F$ is the standard
deviation in the averaging window and $N_\mathrm{eff} = f_F \times 5$~s is
the number of fluctuation cycles it contains (8--87). This was combined in
quadrature with the standard error of the calibration slope. The resulting
within-run uncertainty in $C_D$ has a median of \CdUncMed\% and a maximum
of \CdUncMax\%. As discussed in \S\ref{sec:discussion}, this is smaller
than the observed run-to-run scatter. \todo{Systematic contributions: carriage speed, $L$
(free-surface level and run-up), $D$ tolerance, load-cell
nonlinearity/cross-talk, temperature measurement.}

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.6\textwidth]{cd_st_vs_re.pdf}
  \caption{(a) Drag coefficient and (b) Strouhal number against Reynolds
    number for the three cylinders. Error bars show the within-run
    statistical uncertainty only. The open
    symbol is run~\ExclRun, excluded because of zero drift
    (\S\ref{sec:processing}).}
  \label{fig:cd}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Summary by cylinder. $N$ is the number of accepted runs;
    $\overline{C_D}$ is the mean $\pm$ standard deviation over runs, and
    $\overline{C_D}_{,c}$ is the blockage-corrected mean
    (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).}
  \label{tab:summary}
  \input{generated/summary_table.tex}
\end{table}

The drag coefficients are shown in figure~\ref{fig:cd}(a) and summarized in
table~\ref{tab:summary}. All accepted runs fall within
$1.09 \le C_D \le 1.22$. The mean over all \NGood{} runs is
$C_D = \CdMean \pm \CdSD$. The cylinder means, \CdA, \CdB{} and \CdC{} for
$D = 25.4$, 38.1 and 50.8~mm, differ by less than the run-to-run standard
deviation of each (0.03), so no diameter effect can be resolved. Including
run~\ExclRun{} would have raised the 50.8~mm mean by about 0.02 and
suggested a diameter dependence that is not present in the screened data.

A least-squares fit of the pooled data gives a weak increase of $C_D$ with
Reynolds number, $\mathrm{d}C_D/\mathrm{d}\log_{10}\mathrm{Re} = \CdSlope$.
The data for different diameters overlap where their Reynolds-number ranges
coincide (figure~\ref{fig:cd}a). \todo{Add a confidence interval for the
slope and decide whether to claim the trend.}

The Strouhal number (figure~\ref{fig:cd}b) is $\mathrm{St} = \StMean$ for
every run, with a maximum deviation of \StDev{} across cylinders, speeds
and test series. \todo{This is remarkably tight given the 0.2~Hz
intrinsic resolution of a 5~s window; check against the raw spectra.}

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Comparison with canonical data.}
The measured $C_D \approx \CdMean$ lies at the upper end of the range
reported for nominally two-dimensional cylinders at these Reynolds numbers
\citep{Zdravkovich1997}. The Strouhal number of 0.20 agrees with the
accepted subcritical value \citep{Williamson1996,Norberg2003}. The weak
rise of $C_D$ with Re has the same sign as the gradual increase in the
canonical curve through this range. \todo{Overlay a reference data set
(e.g., digitized from \citet{Zdravkovich1997} or \citet{Norberg2003})
on figure~\ref{fig:cd}.}

\paragraph{Blockage.}
The frontal-area blockage ratio is \BlockageA, \BlockageB{} and
\BlockageC\% for the three diameters. Applying a Maskell-type correction
\citep{Maskell1963}, $C_{D,c} = C_D/(1 + m\,C_D\,S/C)$ with $m = 2.5$,
reduces the cylinder means to \CdCorrA, \CdCorrB{} and \CdCorrC{}
(table~\ref{tab:summary}), and the overall mean to \CdCorrMean. The
correction therefore introduces an ordering with diameter
that the raw data do not show. However, the correction was derived for closed
wind-tunnel test sections, while here the tank is open at the top. A free
surface constrains the flow less than a rigid wall, so the correction is
probably an upper bound. \todo{Choose and justify the correction (Maskell, Allen \& Vincenti, or
the 2D cylinder data of \citet{West1982}) and the value of $m$; decide
whether corrected or uncorrected values are the headline result.}

\paragraph{Aspect ratio and the free surface.}
Blockage and aspect ratio co-vary in this data set: the largest cylinder
has both the highest blockage and the lowest $L/D$ (\LDC, versus \LDA{} for
the smallest). Finite aspect ratio normally reduces $C_D$ below its
two-dimensional value. A free end with a 0.15~m gap to the floor and a
free-surface end at Froude numbers up to \FrMax{} both modify the local
flow near the ends \citep{ChaplinTeigen2003}. With $\mathrm{Fr}$ tied to
$\mathrm{Re}$ for each cylinder, the present data cannot separate
Froude-number effects from Reynolds-number effects. \todo{Add free-surface
observations (wave height, ventilation at high speed) if available.}

\paragraph{Repeatability.}
Even after removing a linear trend in $\log\mathrm{Re}$ for each
cylinder, the residual scatter in $C_D$ (standard deviation 0.02--0.04) is
two to three times the typical within-run uncertainty (\CdUncMed\%). Most
of the scatter therefore comes from run-to-run variation, not from the
finite averaging time. Candidate
sources include residual tank motion from preceding runs (noted in the
run log for run~4), carriage-speed repeatability, and drift in the load
cell mount. Repeat runs at fixed conditions would quantify this
directly. \todo{Add repeat runs or state this as a limitation.}

\section{Conclusions}
\label{sec:conclusions}

Drag and vortex-shedding measurements on three surface-piercing circular
cylinders in a towing tank, over $\mathrm{Re} = \ReMin$--$\ReMax$, give:
\begin{itemize}
  \item a drag coefficient of $C_D = \CdMean \pm \CdSD$, independent of
    diameter within the run-to-run scatter, with a weak increase of
    \CdSlope{} per decade of Re;
  \item a Strouhal number of \StMean, independent of Re and $D$;
  \item a blockage correction of up to about 12\% for the largest cylinder,
    comparable to the expected difference from two-dimensional reference values. The
    correction is therefore the dominant interpretive uncertainty;
  \item a simple zero-return check that identified one compromised run
    which would otherwise have biased the diameter comparison.
\end{itemize}
\todo{Revise once the motivation and blockage treatment are settled.}

